   "content": "<p>… the news reached him, he at once reached Anbar and announced his accession. It is true that the foundation of the power of the Abbasid family was laid by Saffah, but in the short span of four years he did not get the opportunity to strengthen the government. Mansur established the Abbasid caliphate on firm foundations; therefore Mansur alone is considered the real founder of the caliphate of the Banu Abbas. In his era several claimants to the caliphate came forward, but Mansur, defeating them all, laid the foundation of his family&#x27;s government, which remained established for approximately five hundred years.</p><p>The revolt of Abdullah bin Ali:</p><p>Saffah&#x27;s uncle Abdullah bin Ali announced his own caliphate in Syria. Mansur defeated him with the help of Abu Muslim. He took refuge with his brother Sulayman bin Ali, ruler of Basrah, but later Mansur cunningly had him confined in a house standing on foundations of salt. With the collapse of this house he perished.</p><p>The killing of Abu Muslim:</p><p>Mansur was afraid of the growing power of Abu Muslim. He made a plan to remove him from the way. Mansur had called Abu Muslim to Syria for help. So he was offered the governorship of Egypt and Syria so that he could not go to Khurasan. Abu Muslim understood this move and set off towards Khurasan. Mansur sent a few cunning men, called him to the court, and gave him an enthusiastic and warm welcome. But later, by deceit, he had him killed in the court itself by means of his men. This great benefactor of the Banu Abbas reached this terrible end. This is a stain on the character of Mansur. Through the killing of Abu Muslim a tremendous …</p>",
