   "content": "<p>Mahdi&#x27;s reforms:</p><p>Mahdi&#x27;s era was an era of peace. He paid most attention to reforms. He reorganised all the departments afresh. Separate administrators were appointed for them. He established the postal system on modern lines. Between Makkah, Madinah, Yemen and Baghdad he started the series of post by means of fast horses and camels. Stipends were fixed from the Bayt al-Mal for the disabled and the needy. In the same way stipends were fixed for the wives and children of prisoners too. Mahdi paid special attention to roads. A network of roads was laid in the whole country, and travel was made safe. Along the roads inns were built, in which every kind of facility was provided to travellers. The highway built from Qadisiyyah to Makkah passed through Zubalah. Here and there water tanks were built which remained full of water at all times. Mahdi was also fond of making buildings. He had a magnificent palace built on the bank of the river Tigris. He extended the Jami&#x27; Mosque of Basrah. He expanded the Masjid al-Haram. He established a new mint in &#x27;Isa-abad.</p><p>Mahdi&#x27;s character:</p><p>According to one historian, &quot;the prevention of oppression and tyranny, avoidance of killing and bloodshed, helping the frightened and generosity made Mahdi accepted and beloved among the common people and the elite.&quot; According to another historian, &quot;Mahdi&#x27;s reign was very blessed and praiseworthy. Both his character and appearance were good. The subjects loved him.&quot; Mahdi was a God-fearing, pious, good-mannered and generous caliph. He was also an administrator and organiser of a high degree. He would go himself to visit the sick among the poor. The wife of Marwan II, in torn old …</p>",
