   "theory": "… the opponents got the golden opportunity to flourish. So revolts began to take place here and there. But Fadl always kept Ma'mun unaware of the real situation. Ma'mun kept thinking that there was complete peace and order in the country.\n\nThe first period of Ma'mun al-Rashid (in Merv)\n\nRevolt of the army against Tahir:\n\nAfter the conquest of Baghdad, Tahir's army revolted for an increase in salaries, but soon the chiefs of the army themselves asked for pardon and the revolt ended.\n\nRevolt of Nasr bin Shabath:\n\nAmin had the help of the Arabs. The Arab chief of Aleppo, Nasr bin Shabath, revolted joining other Arab chiefs with him. Fadl bin Sahl sent Tahir against him, but he was defeated. Nasr conquered the region of the province of Jazirah. The Ahl al-Bayt too joined Nasr. Nasr remained a rebel for approximately eleven years. At last Tahir's son Abdullah defeated him and sent him to Ma'mun in Baghdad. Ma'mun kept him under house arrest in Madinat al-Mansur.\n\nRevolt and killing of Muhammad bin Ibrahim:\n\nIn 814 CE an 'Alawi elder, Muhammad bin Ibrahim, (began) activities against the Abbasids …",
