   "content": "<p>Abu al-Fadl Ja&#x27;far bin Mu&#x27;tadid al-Muqtadir billah</p><p>908 CE to 932 CE</p><p>Muktafi had nominated his younger brother Ja&#x27;far as his successor. His age was only 13 years. The amirs and other Abbasid chiefs opposed, but the vizier &#x27;Abbas bin Hasan supported him, because his personal interest was linked with him. Allegiance to Ja&#x27;far took place, but the leading Abbasid chiefs were still against him. At the top of the list was the amir Husayn bin Hamdan, wali of Mosul. Husayn killed &#x27;Abbas and handed the seat of the caliphate to Ibn al-Mu&#x27;tazz. Ibn al-Mu&#x27;tazz ascended the throne with the title Murtada billah. During this time Ibn Hamdan went from Baghdad to Mosul. In his absence Muqtadir&#x27;s caliphate was restored. He began retaliatory actions. All those amirs who had opposed Muqtadir&#x27;s caliphate were imprisoned or killed. In 305 AH Husayn bin Hamdan too was killed.</p><p>Revolts and mismanagement:</p><p>The ruler of Armenia and Azerbaijan, amir Ibn Abi al-Saj, began to forcibly take possession of other regions. Muqtadir sent an army and defeated him, and he was arrested. In Rayy mismanagement was increasing. Muqtadir brought it under control. The Saffarid government had become considerably weak. So in 298 AH it was put to an end. During this time the Qaramitah again began plundering. The Abbasid forces were fleeing out of fear of them, but because of a river intervening the Qaramitah went back.</p>",
