   "content": "<p>Chapter: 8</p><p>Abu Mansur Fadl bin Mustazhir al-Mustarshid billah</p><p>1118 CE to 1134 CE</p><p>Mustazhir had nominated his son Abu Mansur as his heir in his very lifetime. In 512 AH Abu Mansur ascended the throne. At that time his age was 27 years. The people of the Abbasid family took allegiance to him. Mustazhir&#x27;s son al-Mustarshid billah was an intelligent, courageous and spirited caliph. He tried to get out of the Seljuq snare and wanted to make the caliphate dignified and powerful once again. Abu Mansur himself took part in wars. The Seljuqs did not want the caliph to restore his position. Differences between the Sultan and Mustarshid kept increasing. At last it came to war between both, but later peace was made between them. In 525 AH Sultan Mahmud passed away. After the Sultan&#x27;s death civil war began among the Seljuqs. At last Sultan Mas&#x27;ud came to power, but tension between them remained and at last it came to war. Sultan Sanjar instructed Mas&#x27;ud that Mustarshid should be sent back honourably, but unfortunately during this time the followers of the Batiniyyah sect killed him in 529 AH. His age was 43 years. According to Suyuti, Mustarshid was a high-spirited, brave and bold caliph. Ibn al-Athir too has written the same. After his death his son Abu Ja&#x27;far Mansur bin Mustarshid al-Rashid billah sat on the throne in 529 AH. He remained in power for approximately 11 months. Sultan Mas&#x27;ud Seljuqi and his …</p>",
