   "content": "<p>… opposition between them increased. At last in 530 AH he was deposed, and his uncle Abu Abdullah Muhammad bin Mustazhir al-Muqtafi li-amr Allah was made caliph. His age was forty years. He too was a brave, valiant and bold ruler. The important event of his era of government is the establishment of the Khwarazmi government. Its founder was Khwarazm Shah. This government was established in the province of Khwarazm. Khwarazm Shah defeated Sultan Sanjar in 536 AH. Sultan Mas&#x27;ud died in 547 AH. With his death the lamp of the Seljuq dynasty began to go out. His brother Sulayman Shah took possession of the throne.</p><p>Death:</p><p>Muqtafi departed from this transient world in 555 AH. After Muqtafi&#x27;s death his son Yusuf ascended the throne. In this era the Seljuq government had become considerably weak; therefore interference from their side decreased considerably. In Yusuf&#x27;s era Nur al-Din Zangi kept fighting against the Christians. The Fatimid caliphs too had become puppets in the hands of the amirs and viziers. The position of the caliphs was in name only; all powers were with the viziers. At last in Egypt Salah al-Din Ayyubi obtained power.</p><p>Death:</p><p>In 566 AH Yusuf was forcibly shut in a bath, where he died of suffocation. After this killing Amir &#x27;Adud al-Din made Abu Muhammad Hasan bin Mustanjid al-Mustadi&#x27; bi-amr Allah caliph and got the vizierate for his own son. Qutb al-Din was made commander-in-chief. The famous event of his era is Salah al-Din Ayyubi&#x27;s taking possession of Egypt. In 567 AH, with the death of the caliph al-&#x27;Adid …</p>",
