   "content": "<p>Hulagu Khan regarded the caliph as a religious leader; therefore he shrank from attacking. In his heart there was a fear of his caliphate, that by provoking him he might be caught in some punishment. But unfortunately, on the advice of Khwajah Nasir al-Din Tusi, he became ready to attack Baghdad. In 656 AH Hulagu Khan attacked Baghdad. A large part of the army had been dismissed, but the army that was present fought back with great bravery and repulsed the initial attacks of the Tatars. But when the Tatars attacked again, defeat had to be faced. With this the destruction of Baghdad began. This was its destiny. The cradle of the world&#x27;s civilisation and culture was looted at the hands of the savage Tatars. The Tatars came as a punishment for the Muslims. From this destruction no sect of the Muslims remained safe. All had to be disgraced and humiliated, because this was the decision of time. The end of those who raise walls of hatred among themselves is destruction. From this destruction men, women, the old, children — no one could escape. For forty days the market of killing, pillage and plunder remained hot. According to Ibn Khaldun, countless wealth was obtained from the royal palaces; all the books of the Abbasid library were drowned in the Tigris. According to one estimate the blood of sixteen lakh human beings was shed. Ibn al-&#x27;Alqami obtained pardon for his own life from Hulagu Khan and had the caliph Musta&#x27;sim killed together with the &#x27;ulama&#x27;, fuqaha&#x27; and other notables. This event took place in Muharram 656 AH. Later Ibn al-&#x27;Alqami too had to face disgrace and humiliation, and died a few days later.</p><p>With the destruction of Baghdad the centrality of the Muslims ended. The Abbasid government was established in Egypt, but these caliphs too were rulers in name only.</p>",
