     "text": "In the last era of the Abbasid dynasty, because of the weakness of the centre, many autonomous governments were born. Mahmud Ghaznavi established a strong government. During this very time a brave nation of Central Asia, the Salajiqah, took possession of the regions of Turkistan and Khurasan and laid the foundation of a magnificent government. After Mahmud's death the famous Turkish chief Tughril Seljuqi conquered the regions of Nishapur, Jurjan, Tabaristan and Khwarazm and included them in his sultanate. Tughril made Rayy his capital. Hamadan, Iraq and Armenia too were included in the Seljuq sultanate. The Seljuqs in respect of belief were Sunni. They greatly respected the Abbasid caliphs. The Daylamis wanted that the Abbasid caliphate too should be included in the Fatimid government of Egypt. The caliph had become fed up with their conspiracies. So Caliph al-Qa'im requested Tughril for help. Tughril accepted this request with pleasure, and in 1055 CE, expelling the Daylamis from Baghdad, gave the caliph deliverance. The caliph, being pleased, gave Tughril the title of Sultan. Tughril, setting the conditions in Baghdad right, went back. In Tughril's era the Seljuqs made great progress.",
