   "content": "<p>In a short time Salah al-Din Ayyubi obtained great fame. After the death of the Fatimid caliph Salah al-Din became the ruler of Egypt and began the Abbasid khutbah. He kept working in the capacity of viceroy of Nur al-Din Zangi. When Nur al-Din Zangi passed away, he included the regions of Hijaz and Yemen too in his sultanate. The successors of Nur al-Din Zangi too accepted the patronage of Salah al-Din. Salah al-Din turned towards the Christians. He presented some conditions, which the Christians rejected. Salah al-Din besieged the city and at the same time made a firm intention of taking revenge for the general massacre of the Muslims. A few days later the people of the city laid down their arms and requested mercy. Salah al-Din&#x27;s heart softened; he gave up the intention of taking bloody revenge. He gave the crusader army forty days&#x27; respite. He paid the ransom of ten thousand soldiers from his own pocket. In 1187 CE Bayt al-Maqdis came again into the possession of the Muslims and remained with them for centuries. But unfortunately in the last century Israel took possession of it.</p><p>3. The Third Crusade:</p><p>With Sultan Salah al-Din Ayyubi&#x27;s possession the priests and rulers set fire throughout Europe against him. To take Bayt al-Maqdis back the Christians began preparations on a wide scale. In this war three great emperors of Europe took part — Richard the Lionheart of England, Frederick of Germany and Philip of France. The armies of all of them began to gather near Acre. At this delicate occasion Salah al-Din tried to obtain help from the Muslim rulers, but …</p>",
