   "content": "<p>Chapter: 11</p><p>The last caliphs … decline of the Abbasid dynasty</p><p>Administration of the sultanate, military system</p><p>The last caliphs of the Banu Abbas (a review)</p><p>Ma&#x27;mun al-Rashid during his illness had nominated his brother Mu&#x27;tasim billah as heir-apparent. After Ma&#x27;mun&#x27;s death Mu&#x27;tasim ascended the throne. He ruled from 833 CE to 845 CE. In respect of learning he was completely blank, but he was very brave and bold. For this reason historians also call him the &quot;soldier caliph&quot;. The special thing of his era is that to suppress the Iranians he began giving concessions to the Turks, which later proved very costly. In the hands of the Turks the caliphs remained mere puppets. Mu&#x27;tasim greatly oppressed Imam Ahmad bin Hanbal (may Allah have mercy on him). He had him flogged, because he was strongly against the Mu&#x27;tazili beliefs. Mu&#x27;tasim died in 227 AH. After him his son al-Wathiq ascended the throne. He was a learned, scholarly ruler. He was very famous in poetry and oratory. He died in 232 AH. Wathiq had not appointed anyone his successor. The amirs selected his brother Mutawakkil &#x27;ala Allah as caliph. Mutawakkil&#x27;s era was an era of peace and prosperity. He himself was extremely pleasure-seeking and of colourful temperament. His era is called the &quot;era of delight&quot;. His …</p>",
