   "theory": "… came out in the form of revolts. From the very beginning of the Banu Abbas opposition to it began. The Banu Abbas ruled for approximately five hundred years. Baghdad became the centre of the world of Islam. It was declared the cradle of learning and civilisation, but at last at the hands of Hulagu Khan it was turned into a heap of rubble. The select causes of the decline of the Banu Abbas are as follows.\n\n1. Pleasure-seeking rulers:\n\nIn the early era of the Banu Abbas only a few caliphs were possessors of high abilities. Through continuous hard work they gave the government stability. Later the caliphs fell into luxury and pleasure. They took no interest of any kind in the affairs of government; rather their nights and days were spent in gatherings of wine-drinking, music and maidens. They kept becoming heedless of the condition of the public. In this way the distances between the public and them increased day by day. The caliphs lost the trust of the public, the result of which came out in the form of destruction.\n\n2. Treatment of the Shi'an-e-'Ali:\n\nIn the beginning the Abbasids, joining with the Shi'an-e-'Ali, had run the movement against the Banu Umayyah, but they themselves took possession of power. Although later they kept trying to bring the Shi'an-e-'Ali along, the distances between them kept increasing. In Mansur's era the series of their revolts began. Now they began to keep watch on their activities. The sympathies of the public were even now mostly with the Shi'an-e-'Ali. In Tabaristan and Egypt governments of the Shi'a were established. Their differences increased so much that Ibn al-'Alqami, inviting Hulagu, provided the opportunity to destroy Baghdad.",
