   "content": "<p>Chapter: 12</p><p>Scholars of the Abbasid era</p><p>1. Hazrat Imam Abu Hanifah Nu&#x27;man bin Thabit</p><p>Hazrat Imam Abu Hanifah is counted among the shining stars of Islamic jurisprudence. He spent most of his life in Kufa. He obtained fame because of istihsan. His name was Nu&#x27;man bin Thabit. He was born in Kufa in 80 AH. Imam Abu Hanifah, because of his learning and scholarly excellences, is called Imam-e-A&#x27;zam. Among his teachers are included Imam Ja&#x27;far Sadiq, Hammad and &#x27;Abd al-Rahman bin Hurmuz. Kufa was the centre of the science of hadith and fiqh. He was a very big cloth merchant. He kept doing the work of teaching independently. He also used to help his pupils financially. His era was the era of Mansur. Among his pupils are included Abu Yusuf and Muhammad bin al-Hasan. Imam Abu Yusuf was the Qadi al-Qudat of Caliph Harun al-Rashid.</p><p>Death:</p><p>He died in Baghdad in 150 AH.</p><p>2. Imam Malik bin Anas</p><p>Hazrat Imam Malik was born in Madinah Munawwarah in 92 AH. Madinah Munawwarah, because of being the centre of the Muhajirin and Ansar, remained a scholarly centre. He was a pupil of &#x27;Abdullah bin &#x27;Umar. Imam Ja&#x27;far Sadiq …</p>",
