   "content": "<p>… and in the south the Strait of Gibraltar is situated. Its length is approximately 1770 kilometres, breadth 966 kilometres, and total area comes to approximately 17 lakh kilometres. Approximately 200 years before the arrival of the Muslims the Visigoth (Goth) nation, defeating the Romans, had established its government here. The Romans were civilised, but the Goths were savage, bloodthirsty and ignorant. Benefiting from the Latins they laid the foundation of their sultanate. They paid special attention to agriculture. A leading military officer said: &quot;Andalus in the beauty of sky and earth resembles Syria; in the delicacy of water and air, Yemen; in perfumes, India; in fertility, Egypt; and in precious minerals (metals), China.&quot;</p><p>Social and communal condition of Andalus before the Muslims:</p><p>According to Stanley Lane-Poole, savage tribes, the Visigoth Goths, were rulers over Andalus. In Andalus the amirs remained drowned in pleasures. Their life was devoted to eating, drinking and other pleasures. The majority of the public consisted of slaves, who kept working day and night in the fields, but nothing of it was their own. With time their masters kept changing. Between these two classes the most oppressed class was that of the slaves, on whom pressure kept falling from both sides. They had to pay the greater part of the country&#x27;s taxes. This class provided the means of pleasure for the amirs. The whole business of government too this class had to run. In society such people had disappeared who could face attackers. The amirs and nobles had drowned in their colourful pleasures. Their swords had become rusty. They were running far away from war. In the country organised and …</p>",
