   "content": "<p>… began to fill. Walid wrote Musa a letter of congratulation and emphasised returning. Musa made his son Abd al-Aziz governor of Andalus. Abd al-Aziz made Cordoba his capital. Musa set off from Andalus with countless precious spoils. The people of Qayrawan gave him a splendid welcome. Musa set off for Damascus with these very spoils. He met Walid in the Jami&#x27; Mosque of Damascus and handed everything over to him. 40 days later Walid bin Abd al-Malik died and Sulayman bin Abd al-Malik ascended the throne. Where Sulayman made other generals the target of his vengeful actions, there Musa too came into their grip. At that time Musa&#x27;s age was 80 years. Inhuman oppressions were inflicted on him, and at last he died rubbing his heels.</p><p>Abd al-Aziz bin Musa divided Andalus into provinces. He enforced various kinds of reforms, so that the subjects could live a prosperous life. The flames of Sulayman&#x27;s vengeance reached Andalus too. Abd al-Aziz was martyred by poison in Rajab 97 AH. Sulayman could not appoint any wali. The Arab amirs appointed Ayyub bin Habib Lakhmi as their amir.</p><p>The central government did not accept Ayyub&#x27;s amirate because he was a relative of Musa. Harb bin Abd al-Rahman (Hurr bin Abd al-Rahman) was made amir. He took over this post in 98 AH. He was an extremely strict and oppressive person. People were displeased with him. Hazrat &#x27;Umar bin Abd al-Aziz nominated Samh bin Malik Khawlani as amir of Andalus. Samh bin Malik Khawlani took over the amirate in 100 AH. He was a God-fearing and pious person. He had the bridge of Cordoba built. The city wall was rebuilt. In 102 AH he attacked France. In this battle Samh was martyred. According to Ibn Khaldun the people of Andalus made Abd al-Rahman bin Abdullah their amir. The governor of Africa sent &#x27;Anbasah as governor. In 107 AH Yahya bin …</p>",
