   "content": "<p>Chapter: 3</p><p>Amir Abd al-Rahman al-Dakhil</p><p>138 AH to 172 AH, corresponding to 756 CE to 788 CE</p><p>When the sun of the power of the Banu Abbas shone and the dew of the Banu Umayyah began to disappear, doomsday fell upon the members of the Banu Umayyah. The supporters of the Banu Abbas began to search out and kill the members of the Banu Umayyah. Among those who, escaping this bloodshed, went to other countries was also Abd al-Rahman bin Mu&#x27;awiyah bin Hisham bin Abd al-Malik. He turned towards Africa. His slave Badr too was with him. The governor of Africa, Abd al-Rahman bin Habib, too had turned against him; therefore he kept taking refuge, going out of one tribe into another tribe. In this way five years passed. After this he stayed in Maliba. According to Ibn Khaldun he sent his slave Badr to those people in Andalus who were sympathisers of the Marwanids. Badr fulfilled his duty in the best manner. He made all of them ready to support Abd al-Rahman. All the people promised with great joy to help Abd al-Rahman. The struggle of the Yamani and Mudari tribes had been continuing for a considerable time. Both tribes were trying to humble each other. The Yamani tribes made a full commitment to support Abd al-Rahman. When Badr became fully certain, he came back to Abd al-Rahman and informed him of all the circumstances. Abd al-Rahman set off in 138 AH. First of all a group of the people of Seville swore allegiance at the hand of Abd al-Rahman. The wali of Sidonia, &#x27;Attab, …</p>",
