   "content": "<p>… were the prominent posts. Separate courts were established for civil and criminal cases. The head of the department of justice was called qadi. Abd al-Rahman included Berbers and mawali in the army. He strengthened the naval fleet. Al-Dakhil himself was also a poet. He patronised the sciences and arts, and Cordoba soon became the cradle of the sciences and arts. He established a mint in Cordoba. According to Allama al-Maqqari people used to stop Abd al-Rahman in public and seek justice. He had many buildings built and orchards planted. The greater part of Abd al-Rahman&#x27;s life passed in continuous struggle.</p><p>Problems and difficulties:</p><p>The mother of the golden-haired, twenty-year-old Abd al-Rahman belonged to the Berber tribes. This was the reason that the Berber chief of Africa supported Abd al-Rahman fully. With their support Abd al-Rahman succeeded in obtaining power in Andalus. Mansur, in spite of failure, spread a net of conspiracies in Spain. The representatives of the Abbasids kept inciting the Berbers and other tribes against Abd al-Rahman. Besides the Muslims the Christians too kept conspiring against Abd al-Rahman. They had established a small state in Asturias in Jabal al-Barakat. Abd al-Rahman remained entangled with the rebel Muslim chiefs; during this time the Christians strengthened their state. The boundaries of this state kept increasing. At last they invited Yusuf&#x27;s son Abu al-Aswad, son-in-law Abd al-Rahman bin Habib and Sulayman, the governor of Barcelona, to invite Charlemagne, ruler of France, to attack Spain. With Sulayman&#x27;s help Charlemagne made a full attack on Spain, but because of internal revolts in his own country he had to return unsuccessful. Later Charlemagne made peace with Abd al-Rahman. In the same way, of the revolts of the Arab chiefs …</p>",
