   "content": "<p>… supervision and the protection of travellers, staff remained present at all times. To supervise the staff Abd al-Rahman himself patrolled. On showing negligence they were punished. During the patrol Abd al-Rahman helped the needy and the poor immediately. In the same way, for the convenience and welfare of the people, decrees were issued on the spot. Al-Dakhil had made special arrangements for the training of his sons. It was necessary for them to go to the offices and courts and review their work and proceedings personally. In this way the princes had to make decisions personally in many government matters.</p><p>Character and achievements:</p><p>A Mughal prince of the Mughal dynasty, after losing everything in his own country, came here and laid the foundation of a magnificent sultanate in the subcontinent of Pakistan and India which remained established for many centuries. That prince was Zahir al-Din Babur. In the same way the Umayyad prince Abd al-Rahman, enduring oppression at the hands of the Banu Abbas, entered Spain, and there with empty hands laid the foundation of the magnificent government of the Banu Umayyah which had been looted at the hands of the Banu Abbas. He established a splendid government of the Banu Umayyah in the West. According to Ibn al-Athir &quot;al-Dakhil acted quickly against rebels. He did not rest. He did not leave his work to others. He stood firm on his opinion. He was brave, generous and munificent. He mostly wore white clothes.&quot; Abd al-Rahman was a far-sighted and able politician. He was a first-rate judge of affairs, and making a psychological assessment he would gather people around himself. Abd al-Rahman&#x27;s personality was attractive. People were drawn to him of themselves. Abd al-Rahman was a first-rate judge of men. Of people …</p>",
