   "content": "<p>… when their governments began to appear to be ending, these people began to send gifts. The rulers of Rome and Constantinople too sent gifts. The vizierate was entrusted to Abd al-Malik. After obtaining countless conquests he adopted the title al-Nasir li-Din Allah. Now in the khutbahs &quot;Amir al-Mu&#x27;minin, Hami-e-Din Abd al-Rahman al-Nasir li-Din&quot; began to be read. He ended all the revolts one after the other.</p><p>In Africa the Fatimid government had been established. Their eyes were on Andalus. Al-Nasir, instead of sending an army, fanned the religious differences. For this purpose the help of the honourable &#x27;ulama&#x27; was obtained. Instead of attacking Andalus the Fatimids began to think of their own protection. Abd al-Rahman had a very large war fleet prepared, which kept patrolling in the Mediterranean Sea and the Strait of Gibraltar. He had many buildings built, among which Madinat al-Zahra&#x27; is especially worth mentioning. According to Mr. Dozy, &quot;Among those rulers of the Banu Umayyah who ruled Andalus, undoubtedly the very first rank should be given to Abd al-Rahman al-Nasir li-Din Allah. The achievements he showed during his government are in reality no less than a miracle. When he ascended the throne, sedition and disorder were raging in the sultanate. The field of war and strife was hot. There was disorder in the country. The states had become autonomous. The Christians of the northern regions were looking for an opportunity. The rulers of Leon and Africa were dreaming of attacks on Andalus. Abd al-Rahman overcame all these difficulties and established a magnificent government.&quot; The splendour and glory of Cordoba began to increase day by day.</p><p>The country had been divided into small pieces. Every person had become the king of the part he occupied. Abd al-Rahman issued a royal decree that whoever disobeyed the order would be killed …</p>",
