   "theory": "… would be killed. A harsh attitude was adopted towards rebels. People had become fed up with the party-kings; therefore Abd al-Rahman's measures were praised. Most chiefs accepted obedience. After approximately eighteen years of destruction and ruin people obtained peace. Abd al-Rahman's style of government was established on the following principles.\n\n1. Only the Sultan himself issued royal decrees. Only the Sultan made the decisions of the affairs of the sultanate. No amir or vizier had the power to make decisions.\n\n2. The number of such people was increased who were well-wishers of the Sultan and who had devotion and sympathy for the Sultan. In this way conspiracies decreased to a considerable extent.\n\n3. Powers were taken away from the old conspiring Arab chiefs.\n\n4. The number of the army was increased. The army was reorganised. For personal protection a special army consisting of Christian and Muslim slaves was established. This army supported the Sultan at every difficult time. Later these slaves were granted special jagirs and given the right to keep their own armies. In this way his special army was increased further.\n\nThrough these measures complete peace and order was established in the country. Abd al-Rahman's special army proved beneficial for him temporarily, but proved destructive for future rulers.\n\nAfter this Abd al-Rahman turned his attention towards the Christian rulers. Against them he first sent small expeditions, but no special benefit resulted. To help King Ordoño II bin Alfonso, the emperor of France and other Christian rulers came. So Abd al-Rahman took command of his army himself and obtained victory. The Christians were given an exemplary …",
