   "content": "<p>8. Al-Khatim (the seal):</p><p>This department held the royal seal. The royal seal was placed on all decrees and orders. When any decree or order was issued it was at once sent to this department. After placing the seal on the decree this department sent it to the departments concerned.</p><p>9. Department of supplies:</p><p>All conquests depended on this department. This department arranged the movement of the armies. Providing rations etc. for the army too was the responsibility of this department. The chief officer of this department was called &quot;Arzaq al-Hiyad&quot;.</p><p>10. Miscellaneous measures:</p><p>The caliph was to a considerable extent an absolute ruler. The caliphate had four big departments: finance, army, judiciary and foreign affairs. The heads of departments were called viziers, but they could not make any decision by themselves. In the Majlis-e-Shura, besides the viziers, amirs too were included. The ministers of the sultanate were given the title Wazir al-Wuzara&#x27;. The Khatib al-Rasa&#x27;il carried on correspondence with other countries. The Sahib al-Ashghal kept the account of the expenses of the country. Among the Arabs there were no hereditary amirs and jagirdars. The system of the amirs of Andalus apparently appears personal, but it was to a considerable extent also democratic. According to the law by which the caliph could punish the qadi, by the same law the qadi could punish the caliph too. In the cities muhtasibs were appointed who supervised trade and the markets. There was an excellent system of post. The chief officer of the post was called Sahib al-Barid. On the highways post-stations were established. For the post horses and camels …</p>",
