   "theory": "… finding out the number and position. Today this is called the O.P., that is, the Observation Post. This was also called the vanguard. These people also prepared maps of the enemy's region.\n\n2. Qalb (centre):\n\nThis was called the middle part of the army. The commander of the army was in this part.\n\n3. Maymanah:\n\nThis was the right-hand part of the army.\n\n4. Maysarah:\n\nThis was the left-hand part of the army.\n\n5. Saqah (rearguard):\n\nThis walked behind for the protection of the army. With the army there were storytellers who told tales of bravery. Along with the army they also received physical training. According to need and occasion changes were also made in the rules of war.\n\nThe provincial system\n\nThe amirs of Andalus had divided Andalus into six provinces. The chief ruler of a province was called wali. He was also the commander of the army stationed in the province. The administration consisted of various departments. The heads of departments were called viziers. The provincial government had wide powers. The police department, the department of justice and the department of jails were directly under the central government. The wali provided the king with a fixed army at the time of need. The supplies and expenses of the army too were the responsibility of the wali.",
