   "theory": "7. Abu Abdullah Muhammad bin Abdullah Sharif al-Idrisi\n\nThe name of al-Idrisi holds a high place in geography. He was born in Ceuta in 493 AH. He belonged to a Sayyid family of Africa. He received education from the University of Cordoba. Impressed by his ability, the king of Sicily, Roger II, invited him to come. Al-Idrisi prepared such a silver globe from which the mountains, valleys, seas and rivers of the world came before one's eyes. The diameter of this globe was approximately six feet and its weight five and a half maunds. Roger gave him considerable rewards. He recorded all his observations in his famous book \"Nuzhat al-Mushtaq fi Ikhtiraq al-Afaq\". In this book there were 69 maps. The mouth of the river Nile too was shown. Many translations of this book have been made. According to S. P. Scott, \"the geographers of the Middle Ages may have gained a thousand fames, but no one's lamp could burn before the sun of al-Idrisi's fame.\"\n\nDeath:\n\nAl-Idrisi died in 1166 CE. He was buried in Sicily itself.\n\n8. Abu Zayd Wali al-Din Abd al-Rahman bin Khaldun\n\nIbn Khaldun was born in Tunis on 1 Ramadan 733 CE. He belonged to the descendants of the Yemeni chief of Kindah, Wa'il bin Hujr. In this family there was an abundance of wealth. The first person of this family entered Andalus at the end of the first century Hijri. He received his early education in Tunis itself. He acquired the science of hadith — the Muwatta', Imam Malik, Sahih Muslim and Sahih Bukhari. Of Maliki fiqh he studied al-Mudawwanah, which consists of four volumes. Grammar, lexicography …",
