   "content": "<p>… began two years before his death. The amir&#x27;s idea was that it would be built in the style of the Jami&#x27; Mosque of Damascus. The amir spent eighty thousand red dinars in two years. The lofty walls are taking the form of a city wall. Near the door is that minaret which heard thousands of adhans and saw lakhs of Muslims offering prayers here. The height of the roof is thirty feet. Beautiful mihrabs and attractive pillars are even today witnesses of the greatness of the Muslims. After al-Dakhil, Hisham too spent the fifth part of the spoils on the decoration and adornment of the mosque. This series continued for seven years. Even after him this series did not stop; rather it is spread over centuries. Every amir added something or other to this mosque. Its completion took place in the era of Caliph Hisham al-Mu&#x27;ayyad billah under the supervision of Muhammad bin Abi &#x27;Amir. Its length is 570 feet and breadth 425 feet. The mihrab was prepared from white marble. The number of doors increased from nine to twenty-one. For women there were separate doors. For the amir of Andalus a gold door had been fixed. Near the mosque a Dar al-Sadaqah was built in which all facilities were provided free for destitute students. With lanterns and wax candles the night took on the scene of day. The number of lamps was approximately seven thousand. But when decline came upon the Muslim government in Andalus and Ferdinand entered Cordoba with his queen, first of all he changed the Jami&#x27; Mosque of Cordoba into an idol-house and, erecting various kinds of memorial statues, trampled the sacred and pure beauty of the mosque.</p><p>3. Madinat al-Zahra&#x27;:</p><p>The beginning of this city was in a romantic manner. This beautiful city was built at the time when the star of the Muslims of Andalus was at the peak of its height. This era was in reality the era of Abd al-Rahman al-Nasir.</p>",
