   "content": "<p>Al-Nasir had a beloved, playful and lively slave-girl, Zahra&#x27;. She requested al-Nasir that a city be settled in her name, in which there would be magnificent palaces and beautiful buildings. Al-Nasir accepted this request and issued the decree for building the city in 936 CE. Abd al-Rahman spent twenty-five years of his era, and al-Hakam fifteen years, on its adornment; with this the beauty of Cordoba was enhanced fourfold. This city was seven furlongs long and five furlongs wide. It is said that when this city was complete, Zahra&#x27; came to see it. She saw marble palaces on one side and an ugly mountain on the other. She complained to al-Nasir about the mountain. So, planting beautiful trees on the mountain, it was made Jabal al-&#x27;Arus. But alas! The city whose building took thirty-five years was destroyed and ruined in only twenty-five years at the hands of the Berber armies. The flames of fire turned it into a heap of ashes.</p><p>4. Qasr al-Hamra&#x27;:</p><p>The series of the art of architecture that Abd al-Rahman al-Dakhil began with al-Rusafah reached its height in the era of government of the Banu Nasr. The building of al-Hamra&#x27; was begun by Muhammad bin al-Ahmar. From this connection it was given the name al-Hamra&#x27;. But some historians think that the special mortar which was used in its building — its colour was red; therefore it was given the name al-Hamra&#x27;. This palace of Granada was in the beginning a fort situated on a hill, which was demolished and al-Hamra&#x27; was built. On one side of it are the snow-clad peaks of the Sierra Nevada, and on the other side a fertile valley in which the river Darro flows winding. The interior part of this palace is the best specimen of stucco work, tile work and engraving. Art …</p>",
